WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.250
Picture an ordinary pine tree.

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:07.285
Someone might offer twenty dollars for it
standing in the woods.

00:00:07.285 --> 00:00:11.730
Someone else could turn its wood into several
hundred dollars of lumber.

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:15.410
And a person with the right building system
might use it to replace thousands

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:17.900
of dollars of purchased framing.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.925
How can all of those descriptions be true?

00:00:20.925 --> 00:00:25.640
This presentation follows one tree through
different uses, then examines a proposed

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:27.340
two-tree dome.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:29.740
The central idea is simple.

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:34.245
The value depends on the work you add, the
function you create, and the costs

00:00:34.245 --> 00:00:35.660
you avoid.

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:38.980
Each number answers a different economic
question.

00:00:40.575 --> 00:00:45.230
Our example starts with a pine about fifteen
inches in diameter at breast height,

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:48.515
the standard reference height for measuring a
trunk.

00:00:48.515 --> 00:00:52.550
The source assumes roughly sixty feet of
useful stem and around thirty-seven

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:54.930
cubic feet of solid wood.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:01:00.890
Trees taper, so this is a yield assumption,
not the volume of a perfect cylinder.

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:05.490
The dome scenario uses two suitable trees,
approximately one hundred twenty

00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:09.950
frame members, and a three-hundred-square-foot
structural shell.

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:13.695
It assigns forty-eight hundred dollars to the
commercial framing function those

00:01:13.695 --> 00:01:15.790
members could replace.

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:19.770
That figure is a working comparison supplied
with the project.

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:23.795
It is not a contractor quote or the cost of a
completed home.

00:01:25.408 --> 00:01:30.533
Stumpage means the price paid for standing
timber before harvesting and transport.

00:01:30.533 --> 00:01:35.728
A buyer still has to fell the tree, pull it to
a loading area, haul it, mill

00:01:35.728 --> 00:01:40.433
it, dry the boards, hold inventory, and find
customers.

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:43.923
Builders must then turn those boards into a
structure.

00:01:43.923 --> 00:01:48.363
The source cites Alabama pine sawtimber at
roughly seventeen to twenty-one dollars

00:01:48.363 --> 00:01:51.993
per ton in the fourth quarter of twenty
twenty-five.

00:01:51.993 --> 00:01:56.288
TimberMart-South confirms a South-wide average
of twenty-three dollars and thirty-four

00:01:56.288 --> 00:02:00.368
cents per ton for the second quarter of twenty
twenty-six.

00:02:00.368 --> 00:02:04.408
The source's roughly six-dollar pulpwood
comparison is close to that quarter's

00:02:04.408 --> 00:02:07.008
five dollars and forty cents.

00:02:07.008 --> 00:02:10.953
These are prices per ton, not prices per tree.

00:02:10.953 --> 00:02:15.093
The example's ten-to-thirty-five-dollar tree
range also depends on its merchantable

00:02:15.093 --> 00:02:18.738
weight, quality, access, and local buyers.

00:02:20.367 --> 00:02:23.352
Here is the complete ladder from the source.

00:02:23.352 --> 00:02:27.012
Standing timber might bring ten to thirty-five
dollars.

00:02:27.012 --> 00:02:30.882
Retail-equivalent firewood might represent
about one hundred to one hundred

00:02:30.882 --> 00:02:32.847
thirty-five dollars.

00:02:32.847 --> 00:02:36.727
Rough pine boards could represent three
hundred thirty to four hundred fifteen

00:02:36.727 --> 00:02:38.112
dollars.

00:02:38.112 --> 00:02:42.287
About eighty wedge blanks carry an estimated
two-hundred-fifty-to-four-hundred-dollar

00:02:42.287 --> 00:02:44.677
lumber replacement value.

00:02:44.677 --> 00:02:49.437
In a useful dome assembly, the proposed
contribution rises to twenty-four hundred

00:02:49.437 --> 00:02:52.312
to thirty-two hundred dollars per tree.

00:02:52.312 --> 00:02:56.817
Including the assumed financing, the source
rounds the corresponding lifetime

00:02:56.817 --> 00:03:01.152
payments to fifty-five hundred through
seventy-three hundred dollars.

00:03:01.152 --> 00:03:05.292
The upper figures depend on more favorable
system assumptions.

00:03:05.292 --> 00:03:09.152
We will use the conservative two-tree
allocation of twenty-four hundred dollars

00:03:09.152 --> 00:03:11.622
for the rest of the explanation.

00:03:11.622 --> 00:03:14.402
These are alternative valuations.

00:03:14.402 --> 00:03:18.397
Adding the rows together would count the same
material repeatedly.

00:03:19.992 --> 00:03:24.257
Before pricing firewood, we need to understand
a cord.

00:03:24.257 --> 00:03:29.162
A standard cord occupies one hundred twenty-
eight cubic feet when stacked, commonly

00:03:29.162 --> 00:03:33.512
four feet high, four feet deep, and eight feet
long.

00:03:33.512 --> 00:03:37.237
That stack includes empty spaces between
pieces.

00:03:37.237 --> 00:03:39.822
Solid wood volume is smaller.

00:03:39.822 --> 00:03:44.192
Our model uses about ninety cubic feet of
solid wood per cord.

00:03:44.192 --> 00:03:48.962
Forestry references show that the actual
amount varies with piece size and stacking,

00:03:48.962 --> 00:03:53.962
so ninety is a conversion assumption rather
than a universal physical constant.

00:03:53.962 --> 00:03:57.982
The supplied yield examples place a fifteen-
inch pine near point three five

00:03:57.982 --> 00:04:02.882
cord at sixty feet and point four seven cord
at eighty feet, depending on species

00:04:02.882 --> 00:04:05.252
and usable top diameter.

00:04:05.252 --> 00:04:10.642
For the representative stem, we use point
three five to point four five cord.

00:04:12.242 --> 00:04:17.637
A Tuscaloosa-area seller, North River
Firewood, lists a full cord at two hundred

00:04:17.637 --> 00:04:22.107
seventy-five dollars for pickup and three
hundred dollars delivered, with stacking

00:04:22.107 --> 00:04:24.987
charges and delivery terms to check.

00:04:24.987 --> 00:04:28.962
The source also gives a broader Southeast
comparison of two hundred to three

00:04:28.962 --> 00:04:31.817
hundred twenty-five dollars per cord.

00:04:31.817 --> 00:04:35.982
Using the local two-hundred-seventy-five-to-
three-hundred-dollar benchmark,

00:04:35.982 --> 00:04:40.097
point three five to point four five cord works
out to roughly ninety-five to

00:04:40.097 --> 00:04:43.302
one hundred thirty-five dollars for one tree.

00:04:43.302 --> 00:04:47.692
Two equivalent trees represent about point
seven to point nine cord, or one

00:04:47.692 --> 00:04:51.142
hundred ninety to two hundred seventy dollars.

00:04:51.142 --> 00:04:54.067
This is a retail-equivalent comparison.

00:04:54.067 --> 00:04:58.607
Species, seasoning, and delivery affect actual
sale value.

00:04:58.607 --> 00:05:03.662
Felling, cutting, splitting, seasoning, and
stacking also consume

00:05:03.662 --> 00:05:05.302
time and money.

00:05:05.302 --> 00:05:08.337
The selling price is not the seller's profit.

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:13.675
Now compare those same two trees with the
proposed dome.

00:05:13.675 --> 00:05:18.435
The model assigns them forty-eight hundred
dollars of framing substitution.

00:05:18.435 --> 00:05:22.235
Divide that by the firewood comparison and the
ratio is roughly eighteen to

00:05:22.235 --> 00:05:23.910
twenty-five.

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:27.960
Using two hundred fifty dollars as a simple
midpoint gives forty-eight hundred

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:32.255
divided by two hundred fifty, or nineteen
point two.

00:05:32.255 --> 00:05:34.980
The amount of wood has not increased.

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:37.980
The design gives the wood a different purpose.

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:41.855
The comparison also changes economic
categories.

00:05:41.855 --> 00:05:45.295
Firewood is a processed product you might
sell.

00:05:45.295 --> 00:05:50.035
The dome number is a purchased structural
function you might avoid paying for.

00:05:50.035 --> 00:05:54.150
It becomes a real advantage only if the
substitute actually does the required

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:56.905
job at an acceptable total cost.

00:05:58.533 --> 00:06:02.288
Milling creates another useful product:
boards.

00:06:02.288 --> 00:06:06.783
A board foot is a volume unit equivalent to a
board one inch thick, twelve inches

00:06:06.783 --> 00:06:09.643
wide, and twelve inches long.

00:06:09.643 --> 00:06:13.738
You cannot turn every cubic inch of a log into
standard lumber.

00:06:13.738 --> 00:06:16.963
The saw blade removes a strip called the kerf.

00:06:16.963 --> 00:06:23.238
Outer slabs, edging, defects, and dimensional
requirements reduce recovery.

00:06:23.238 --> 00:06:26.988
The source gives a southern-pine recovery
range of seven point five to eight

00:06:26.988 --> 00:06:30.388
point four board feet per cubic foot of log.

00:06:30.388 --> 00:06:33.723
We use seven point five as the working factor.

00:06:33.723 --> 00:06:38.463
Thirty-seven times seven point five is two
hundred seventy-seven point five,

00:06:38.463 --> 00:06:42.458
rounded here to about two hundred seventy-five
board feet.

00:06:42.458 --> 00:06:46.903
At the assumed dollar twenty to dollar fifty
per board foot, the result is roughly

00:06:46.903 --> 00:06:50.558
three hundred thirty to four hundred thirteen
dollars.

00:06:50.558 --> 00:06:55.293
Mill configuration, grade, and local prices
can change the result.

00:06:56.908 --> 00:07:00.843
The wedge proposal changes the shape of the
desired product.

00:07:00.843 --> 00:07:05.398
A conventional sawmill aims to recover
standard rectangular boards.

00:07:05.398 --> 00:07:10.273
This method divides the log lengthwise into
two halves, then four quarters,

00:07:10.273 --> 00:07:12.113
then eight sectors.

00:07:12.113 --> 00:07:15.313
Each sector has a wedge-shaped cross-section.

00:07:15.313 --> 00:07:19.798
Because the structural design can use that
shape, less wood may need to be removed

00:07:19.798 --> 00:07:22.593
simply to make rectangular stock.

00:07:22.593 --> 00:07:25.618
That is the proposed material advantage.

00:07:25.618 --> 00:07:30.873
It does not eliminate losses from cutting,
defects, taper, trimming,

00:07:30.873 --> 00:07:32.343
or drying.

00:07:32.343 --> 00:07:36.228
The meaningful question becomes how much
usable building structure you can recover

00:07:36.228 --> 00:07:37.773
from the tree.

00:07:37.773 --> 00:07:42.978
To answer it, the resulting pieces must meet
the design's length, cross-section,

00:07:42.978 --> 00:07:46.108
connection, and material requirements.

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:51.380
The source's member-count arithmetic is
straightforward.

00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:55.635
Eight longitudinal sectors over sixty useful
feet give four hundred eighty linear

00:07:55.635 --> 00:07:57.595
feet of wedge stock.

00:07:57.595 --> 00:08:02.030
Divide by six feet per blank and you get
eighty blanks from one tree.

00:08:02.030 --> 00:08:06.205
Two comparable trees therefore offer a
theoretical one hundred sixty six-foot

00:08:06.205 --> 00:08:07.675
blanks.

00:08:07.675 --> 00:08:12.585
This is an inventory calculation, not a
measured production result.

00:08:12.585 --> 00:08:16.760
It assumes the useful stem can supply all
eight acceptable sectors at each needed

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:20.950
section and that the length allowance covers
the actual cutting plan.

00:08:20.950 --> 00:08:22.980
Real trunks taper.

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:28.010
Knots, splits, saw kerf, end trimming, and
drying can turn

00:08:28.010 --> 00:08:31.710
a theoretical blank into a shorter piece or a
reject.

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:36.475
Those effects determine whether the proposed
inventory holds up in practice.

00:08:38.075 --> 00:08:42.455
Against a requirement of about one hundred
twenty members, one hundred sixty

00:08:42.455 --> 00:08:46.075
theoretical blanks leave forty potential
pieces.

00:08:46.075 --> 00:08:51.660
The source identifies several uses for that
allowance: rejects, shorter members,

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:57.045
flooring or support material, bracing, door
framing, test pieces,

00:08:57.045 --> 00:08:59.170
and replacement stock.

00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:03.425
Those forty pieces cannot all be promised as
useful extras.

00:09:03.425 --> 00:09:07.760
Some may be consumed by the losses needed to
obtain the main frame.

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:12.040
Still, the allowance makes the planning
question concrete.

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:16.525
A sawmill asks how much standardized lumber a
log can produce.

00:09:16.525 --> 00:09:20.385
This building method asks how much of the
proposed structure the available stock

00:09:20.385 --> 00:09:21.955
can supply.

00:09:21.955 --> 00:09:26.415
A measured cut list and an inventory of
accepted pieces connect those two questions

00:09:26.415 --> 00:09:27.865
to reality.

00:09:29.492 --> 00:09:34.422
A pile of wedge blanks has a material
substitution value, estimated in the source

00:09:34.422 --> 00:09:38.187
at two hundred fifty to four hundred dollars
per tree.

00:09:38.187 --> 00:09:42.717
The larger economic claim appears when
geometry, a jig, assembly work, and a

00:09:42.717 --> 00:09:47.277
suitable structural design turn those pieces
into a functioning shell.

00:09:47.277 --> 00:09:52.117
A jig is a fixture that holds parts in a
repeatable position while you work.

00:09:52.117 --> 00:09:56.742
It can reduce repeated measuring and help
produce consistent assemblies.

00:09:56.742 --> 00:10:02.007
The transformation therefore belongs to a
system: tree, tools, labor, knowledge,

00:10:02.007 --> 00:10:05.247
organization, and verified performance.

00:10:05.247 --> 00:10:08.722
Assigning all of the resulting value to the
raw tree would hide most of the

00:10:08.722 --> 00:10:11.402
inputs that made the result possible.

00:10:13.033 --> 00:10:18.443
Imagine the conventional route: tree, logger,
truck, sawmill, kiln, wholesaler,

00:10:18.443 --> 00:10:21.728
retailer, framer, and finally a house.

00:10:21.728 --> 00:10:27.153
Now imagine the proposed local route: tree,
radial processing, drying,

00:10:27.153 --> 00:10:30.958
jig, triangular assembly, and dome.

00:10:30.958 --> 00:10:35.258
Each commercial step normally carries
someone's labor, equipment, overhead,

00:10:35.258 --> 00:10:39.368
transportation, inventory, profit, and
financing.

00:10:39.368 --> 00:10:43.423
Performing more of the chain yourself may
reduce some of those purchased services

00:10:43.423 --> 00:10:45.023
and margins.

00:10:45.023 --> 00:10:47.383
The physical work remains.

00:10:47.383 --> 00:10:52.258
You take responsibility for the parts of the
process you can perform effectively.

00:10:52.258 --> 00:10:57.408
The savings come from organizing that work
well, using the available wood efficiently,

00:10:57.408 --> 00:11:02.008
and avoiding costs that your particular
situation does not require.

00:11:02.008 --> 00:11:06.483
Commercial operations also have efficiencies,
so a shorter route alone does

00:11:06.483 --> 00:11:09.508
not prove that your total cost will be lower.

00:11:11.117 --> 00:11:14.472
The landowner might describe a twenty-dollar
tree.

00:11:14.472 --> 00:11:19.112
The firewood seller sees roughly one hundred
dollars of prepared fuel.

00:11:19.112 --> 00:11:22.617
The sawmill sees several hundred dollars of
boards.

00:11:22.617 --> 00:11:28.327
The lumberyard adds drying, grading, stocking,
and retail service.

00:11:28.327 --> 00:11:32.447
The framer turns those boards into an
installed structure that represents thousands

00:11:32.447 --> 00:11:34.882
of dollars of completed work.

00:11:34.882 --> 00:11:39.147
If a customer borrows for that work, the
lender receives payments that include

00:11:39.147 --> 00:11:41.122
interest over time.

00:11:41.122 --> 00:11:44.827
All of these descriptions begin with the same
cellulose.

00:11:44.827 --> 00:11:50.172
Processing, knowledge, organization, and
usefulness change what the next person

00:11:50.172 --> 00:11:51.667
can offer.

00:11:51.667 --> 00:11:55.802
The numbers become understandable once you
identify who owns the next process

00:11:55.802 --> 00:11:59.102
and what that person's price actually
includes.

00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:05.245
With two trees, the complete comparison
becomes easier to see.

00:12:05.245 --> 00:12:09.460
Selling them standing might bring twenty to
seventy dollars combined, although

00:12:09.460 --> 00:12:12.950
a buyer may not want to harvest only two
trees.

00:12:12.950 --> 00:12:16.810
Processing them as firewood gives a retail-
equivalent one hundred ninety to

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:19.335
two hundred seventy dollars.

00:12:19.335 --> 00:12:23.565
Milling them yields about five hundred fifty
board feet in the example, worth

00:12:23.565 --> 00:12:27.715
six hundred sixty to eight hundred twenty-five
dollars at the assumed rough-pine

00:12:27.715 --> 00:12:29.095
price.

00:12:29.095 --> 00:12:32.635
Using them in the dome is modeled to replace
forty-eight hundred dollars of

00:12:32.635 --> 00:12:33.985
framing.

00:12:33.985 --> 00:12:37.800
If that entire amount would otherwise have
been financed under the stated mortgage

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:42.645
assumptions, its lifetime principal-and-
interest payments total about ten thousand

00:12:42.645 --> 00:12:45.310
nine hundred twenty-two dollars.

00:12:45.310 --> 00:12:49.910
These rows measure different alternatives, not
cumulative income.

00:12:51.533 --> 00:12:54.653
Here is what financing adds to the comparison.

00:12:54.653 --> 00:12:58.163
Assume forty-eight hundred dollars would have
been borrowed at a fixed annual

00:12:58.163 --> 00:13:03.203
rate of six point five percent for thirty
years, with monthly payments.

00:13:03.203 --> 00:13:07.253
The standard amortization calculation gives
about thirty dollars and thirty-four

00:13:07.253 --> 00:13:09.083
cents each month.

00:13:09.083 --> 00:13:14.168
Across three hundred sixty payments, the
unrounded calculation totals ten thousand

00:13:14.168 --> 00:13:16.828
nine hundred twenty-two dollars.

00:13:16.828 --> 00:13:21.823
About six thousand one hundred twenty-two
dollars of that total is interest.

00:13:21.823 --> 00:13:26.073
This scenario assumes the balance stays in
place for the full term, with no

00:13:26.073 --> 00:13:28.708
early repayment or refinancing.

00:13:28.708 --> 00:13:32.368
It excludes taxes, insurance, and fees.

00:13:32.368 --> 00:13:36.473
Nominal means we add future dollars without
adjusting for inflation or the time

00:13:36.473 --> 00:13:38.208
value of money.

00:13:38.208 --> 00:13:42.623
The forty-eight hundred dollars is already
included in the payment total.

00:13:42.623 --> 00:13:45.518
Adding it again would double count the
principal.

00:13:47.117 --> 00:13:51.987
Firewood converts wood into heat and consumes
the material in the process.

00:13:51.987 --> 00:13:56.462
Construction can keep that wood in service as
part of a durable asset.

00:13:56.462 --> 00:14:01.177
Using the two-hundred-fifty-dollar firewood
illustration, the forty-eight-hundred-dollar

00:14:01.177 --> 00:14:05.387
framing comparison is nineteen point two times
as large.

00:14:05.387 --> 00:14:08.812
But the deeper distinction is the continuing
function.

00:14:08.812 --> 00:14:13.737
A completed, useful building can provide
shelter and enclosed space, and may

00:14:13.737 --> 00:14:17.187
contribute to property value and owner equity.

00:14:17.187 --> 00:14:20.942
Appreciation is possible, not guaranteed.

00:14:20.942 --> 00:14:25.777
Framing by itself does not establish a
finished building's market value.

00:14:25.777 --> 00:14:30.367
Connections, weather protection, foundations,
services, maintenance, and the

00:14:30.367 --> 00:14:32.912
rest of the project still matter.

00:14:32.912 --> 00:14:36.972
The economic opportunity is to preserve wood
in a useful capital asset whose

00:14:36.972 --> 00:14:39.872
service lasts beyond a single use.

00:14:41.492 --> 00:14:44.762
You can summarize the ladder with a rough
index.

00:14:44.762 --> 00:14:48.037
Set a representative standing value to one.

00:14:48.037 --> 00:14:52.787
The source describes firewood near five times
that value, lumber near fifteen

00:14:52.787 --> 00:14:57.737
to twenty times, the dome contribution around
one hundred times or more, and

00:14:57.737 --> 00:15:02.267
nominal financed payments around two hundred
fifty times or more.

00:15:02.267 --> 00:15:05.497
These are broad economic-use comparisons.

00:15:05.497 --> 00:15:10.217
For example, an exact twenty-dollar baseline
makes a twenty-four-hundred-dollar

00:15:10.217 --> 00:15:14.472
structural contribution one hundred twenty
times as large.

00:15:14.472 --> 00:15:18.117
Changing the starting tree value changes the
ratio.

00:15:18.117 --> 00:15:22.612
The financing row also spans thirty years,
while the material rows describe

00:15:22.612 --> 00:15:25.127
value at one point in time.

00:15:25.127 --> 00:15:29.597
An index makes the scale memorable, but it
should always travel with its assumptions

00:15:29.597 --> 00:15:32.367
and the name of the quantity being compared.

00:15:33.992 --> 00:15:38.907
Suppose the two standing trees together have a
timber value of fifty dollars.

00:15:38.907 --> 00:15:42.747
Forty-eight hundred divided by fifty is
ninety-six.

00:15:42.747 --> 00:15:46.992
That gives a gross raw-material ratio of
ninety-six dollars of framing function

00:15:46.992 --> 00:15:49.997
for each dollar of standing timber value.

00:15:49.997 --> 00:15:52.032
Now include the work.

00:15:52.032 --> 00:15:56.437
If the transformation takes eighty hours,
forty-eight hundred divided by eighty

00:15:56.437 --> 00:15:58.762
is sixty dollars per hour.

00:15:58.762 --> 00:16:02.332
This is gross framing substitution per work
hour.

00:16:02.332 --> 00:16:06.937
It is not a take-home wage or a profit rate,
because it has not deducted cash

00:16:06.937 --> 00:16:09.672
expenses or the value of the timber.

00:16:09.672 --> 00:16:12.547
Both measures can be useful at once.

00:16:12.547 --> 00:16:16.067
One describes the relationship to the raw
material.

00:16:16.067 --> 00:16:19.237
The other describes output relative to time.

00:16:19.237 --> 00:16:22.772
Neither justifies treating the other inputs as
free.

00:16:24.367 --> 00:16:28.257
Under the two-tree assumption, divide the
forty-eight-hundred-dollar framing

00:16:28.257 --> 00:16:30.052
value by two.

00:16:30.052 --> 00:16:35.357
Each tree contributes twenty-four hundred
dollars of gross structural substitution.

00:16:35.357 --> 00:16:39.582
Divide the lifetime payment stream by two and
each tree accounts for about fifty-four

00:16:39.582 --> 00:16:42.947
hundred sixty-one dollars of nominal payments.

00:16:42.947 --> 00:16:47.327
You can therefore describe the same tree as
twenty-dollar standing timber, roughly

00:16:47.327 --> 00:16:53.192
one-hundred-dollar firewood, roughly three-
hundred-fifty-dollar lumber, a twenty-four-
hundred-dollar

00:16:53.192 --> 00:16:57.937
framing contribution, or a fifty-four-hundred-
sixty-one-dollar share of financed

00:16:57.937 --> 00:16:59.362
payments.

00:16:59.362 --> 00:17:02.867
There is no contradiction once you name the
measurement.

00:17:02.867 --> 00:17:06.937
There is also no new market in which someone
automatically pays those larger

00:17:06.937 --> 00:17:09.062
amounts for a raw tree.

00:17:09.062 --> 00:17:13.462
The larger values belong to a successful use
of the complete system.

00:17:15.075 --> 00:17:19.950
Market sale value is what another person is
willing to pay you for the resource.

00:17:19.950 --> 00:17:24.095
Personal use value asks what you can
accomplish by keeping it.

00:17:24.095 --> 00:17:28.375
Suppose someone offers twenty-five dollars for
a standing pine.

00:17:28.375 --> 00:17:31.955
If you can realistically turn it into the
equivalent of twenty-four hundred

00:17:31.955 --> 00:17:36.360
dollars of purchased framing, keeping it may
be the better choice.

00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:40.610
That conclusion depends on having the time,
ability, suitable equipment, and

00:17:40.610 --> 00:17:43.980
legal and structural means to complete the
work.

00:17:43.980 --> 00:17:47.990
It also depends on what you give up by
choosing this project.

00:17:47.990 --> 00:17:52.490
The meaningful opportunity is the highest net
value you can realistically create,

00:17:52.490 --> 00:17:57.055
after paying the processing costs and allowing
for the value of your time.

00:17:57.055 --> 00:18:01.390
A large gross replacement number alone does
not settle the choice.

00:18:02.992 --> 00:18:08.237
The formula is the commercial function
displaced, minus cash processing costs,

00:18:08.237 --> 00:18:11.222
minus the opportunity cost of labor.

00:18:11.222 --> 00:18:16.247
Cash costs can include harvesting, transport,
consumables, tool

00:18:16.247 --> 00:18:21.397
wear, drying, and the other expenses required
for the substitute.

00:18:21.397 --> 00:18:25.147
Count each cost once and compare equivalent
scopes.

00:18:25.147 --> 00:18:29.067
Opportunity cost means the value of the best
realistic alternative you give

00:18:29.067 --> 00:18:30.202
up.

00:18:30.202 --> 00:18:34.537
If forty hours on the tree replace forty hours
of available paid work, your

00:18:34.537 --> 00:18:38.387
after-tax earnings from that work are a useful
benchmark.

00:18:38.387 --> 00:18:43.792
If the alternative is different, choose a
value that reflects your circumstances.

00:18:43.792 --> 00:18:48.002
Finally, compare the resulting net use value
with the amount you could receive

00:18:48.002 --> 00:18:51.532
by selling the tree and with any other
realistic use.

00:18:53.158 --> 00:18:57.783
Let one tree contribute twenty-four hundred
dollars of equivalent framing.

00:18:57.783 --> 00:19:02.803
Suppose direct expenses are three hundred
dollars, the work takes forty hours,

00:19:02.803 --> 00:19:07.238
and your alternative after-tax wage is twenty-
five dollars per hour.

00:19:07.238 --> 00:19:11.753
Forty times twenty-five assigns one thousand
dollars to your time.

00:19:11.753 --> 00:19:16.883
Subtract that and the three hundred dollars of
cash costs from twenty-four hundred.

00:19:16.883 --> 00:19:21.113
The result is eleven hundred dollars of net
personal use value.

00:19:21.113 --> 00:19:25.513
This is the source's worked example, with your
labor fully counted at the assumed

00:19:25.513 --> 00:19:26.743
rate.

00:19:26.743 --> 00:19:31.148
To choose between keeping the tree and
accepting a twenty-five-dollar sale offer,

00:19:31.148 --> 00:19:34.523
subtract that forgone sale value as well.

00:19:34.523 --> 00:19:38.603
The advantage of processing rather than
selling is then one thousand seventy-five

00:19:38.603 --> 00:19:40.003
dollars.

00:19:40.003 --> 00:19:44.558
That final comparison makes the opportunity
cost of the tree explicit.

00:19:46.158 --> 00:19:50.768
The model is most useful when you know which
inputs can change the answer.

00:19:50.768 --> 00:19:54.328
A lower accepted yield may require more trees.

00:19:54.328 --> 00:19:58.058
Higher tool or drying costs reduce the
advantage.

00:19:58.058 --> 00:20:03.818
More hours, or a more valuable alternative use
of your time, also reduce it.

00:20:03.818 --> 00:20:07.988
A lower commercial framing price reduces what
you avoid buying.

00:20:07.988 --> 00:20:12.398
In the worked example, twenty-four hundred
minus three hundred leaves twenty-one

00:20:12.398 --> 00:20:16.528
hundred dollars available to compensate forty
hours of work.

00:20:16.528 --> 00:20:20.493
That is fifty-two dollars and fifty cents per
hour before counting the forgone

00:20:20.493 --> 00:20:21.773
sale.

00:20:21.773 --> 00:20:27.023
Above that opportunity cost, the stated net
use value becomes negative.

00:20:27.023 --> 00:20:31.508
Financing benefits also fall if you borrow
less, repay early, or would not have

00:20:31.508 --> 00:20:33.178
borrowed at all.

00:20:33.178 --> 00:20:37.488
Measuring these inputs turns the idea into an
economic decision.

00:20:39.117 --> 00:20:43.602
A clear public explanation names both the raw
input and the function.

00:20:43.602 --> 00:20:48.007
You could say: In this model, a twenty-dollar
standing tree could contribute

00:20:48.007 --> 00:20:52.337
about twenty-four hundred dollars of avoided
commercial framing cost, before

00:20:52.337 --> 00:20:55.742
processing expenses and the value of my labor.

00:20:55.742 --> 00:20:59.947
That wording is stronger than calling it a
five-thousand-dollar tree, because

00:20:59.947 --> 00:21:03.727
it tells the audience where the value actually
comes from.

00:21:03.727 --> 00:21:08.897
Geometry and a repeatable method let ordinary
material perform a valuable job.

00:21:08.897 --> 00:21:14.057
The labor, tools, knowledge, and design
deserve their share of the explanation.

00:21:14.057 --> 00:21:18.072
Any financed-payment comparison should appear
separately, with the interest

00:21:18.072 --> 00:21:20.447
rate and time period attached.

00:21:22.075 --> 00:21:26.355
We began with an ordinary pine and followed
several possibilities.

00:21:26.355 --> 00:21:31.880
Standing timber, firewood, and lumber have
different processed-material values.

00:21:31.880 --> 00:21:36.120
A successful dome system could use that
material to avoid buying a more expensive

00:21:36.120 --> 00:21:38.125
structural function.

00:21:38.125 --> 00:21:42.465
Avoiding a loan for that function could also
reduce future payments.

00:21:42.465 --> 00:21:46.795
Those claims can coexist because each measures
something different.

00:21:46.795 --> 00:21:50.890
The decisive number for the builder is the
highest realistic net use value after

00:21:50.890 --> 00:21:55.760
expenses, the value of time, and the
alternatives given up.

00:21:55.760 --> 00:21:59.455
That is why the two-tree idea deserves a
careful trial.

00:21:59.455 --> 00:22:04.220
Its promise is to make structural shelter a
valuable use of ordinary wood.

00:22:04.220 --> 00:22:08.635
Measured yield, repeatable work, and an
equivalent functioning structure are

00:22:08.635 --> 00:22:12.125
what would turn that promise into a
demonstrated result.
